{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>To make a distinction between the unclean and between the clean</b> — that we distinguish among all those mentioned between the unclean and the clean regarding the matter of impurity. \"And between the living creature that may be eaten\" — this is drawn after the soul [creature] mentioned, [meaning:] between the soul of the living creature that may be eaten. And it says that we distinguish regarding the matter of eating between the soul of the living creature that may be eaten according to the Torah in the waters and upon the earth, and between the living creature that may not be eaten among them. And in Torat Kohanim (end of Parashat Shemini) they said: The Sages say: \"the living creature that may not be eaten\" — to give a warning for the living creature, and if so it is a negative commandment. And the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 11:47): \"between the unclean and between the clean\" — does one need to say between a cow and a donkey? Are they not already explicit? Rather, between what is unclean to you and clean to you — between one whose half of the windpipe was slaughtered and one whose majority was slaughtered (Sifra). And it was taught there (chapter 12, halakhah 7): And how much is [the difference] between its majority and its half? The width of a hair. And do not be troubled by what they said in the Gemara (Ḥullin 29a) that \"a majority visible to the eyes is required,\" for its meaning is only to exclude the view of the one who says (there) that half-and-half is like a majority. Therefore they said that the slaughtered portion must be an actual majority, such that it is visible to the eyes — not the half that we reckon in our heart and call it \"the majority is slaughtered\" since what was not slaughtered is not more than it. But anything from which one slaughters more than half is valid, even by [the width of] a thread of a hair, as is explicit in that baraita. And so too in the Gemara it emerges thus."]